From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AltarsCN <337293323+Altars3668@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 28 Sep 2026 17:02:14 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] windowscodecs: Decode WebP, as Windows does.

Windows 11 has a WebP decoder in WIC, and Office inserts a WebP picture
through it -- once it has found the transform Windows' WebP image
extension registers, which is how Office tells that WebP can be decoded:
without it the insert silently gives nothing.  Measured on Windows 11
build 29671 (tools/wicwebpprobe, tools/mftwebpprobe):

- The decoder gives every frame in 32bppRGBA at 72 dpi, the canvas as
  libwebp's animation decoder composes it, a still picture being an
  animation of one frame; the pixels are byte for byte libwebp's.
- A still picture is taken on the strength of its header and only
  decoded for its pixels, failing then with MF_E_INVALID_FILE_FORMAT; an
  animation has to be whole from the start (E_UNEXPECTED).  GetFrame
  takes any index, copying a missing frame fails with MF_E_INVALIDINDEX.
- An animation's loop count and each frame's duration are its metadata,
  through readers of the ANIM and ANMF chunks that have component
  information but no COM registration.
- The transform is a video decoder for the subtype that is the WIC
  decoder's CLSID, out to P010, named WebpImageExtension; every method
  but GetAttributes is E_NOTIMPL.

Beside it, the component information now answers as Windows does: the
four DoesSupport* of each codec, DoesRequireFixedSize of the metadata
handlers, the signing status (always signed) and the specification
version, which were stubs or missing; the DDS encoder is registered, and
the handlers' names and flags are corrected.  The tests pass on
Windows.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
(cherry picked from commit 2330bd8f5a1332493a8a236d3b9c916e9d1cc098)

